    "result_text": "All 11 test classes are the acceptance checkpoints, and all pass. The implementation is complete.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented the `sharesHousehold` / `householdId` feature in the Spring PetClinic REST app, following the existing conventions used for the earlier household-duplicate and `customerCode` rules.\n\n**Behavior:** On `POST /api/owners`, when another owner already exists with the same last name and address:\n- Without `sharesHousehold: true` \u2192 still rejected with `409 Conflict` (Cp10, unchanged).\n- With `sharesHousehold: true` \u2192 the owner is allowed, and the new owner plus every existing member of that household are assigned the **same stable `householdId`**, which is returned on `GET /api/owners/{id}`.\n\nThe `householdId` is a deterministic function of the normalized last name + address (first 12 upper-case hex chars of SHA-256), so every owner in the same household independently derives the identical value \u2014 a genuinely stable shared identifier.\n\n**Changes:**\n- `openapi.yml` \u2014 added the read-only `householdId` property to the `Owner` schema (regenerates `OwnerDto`; MapStruct auto-maps it by name).\n- `model/Owner.java` \u2014 added the persisted `householdId` field with getter/setter.\n- `db/{hsqldb,h2,postgres,mysql}/schema.sql` \u2014 added the `household_id` column.\n- `rest/controller/v1/OwnerRestControllerV1.java` \u2014 reworked the household check to compute the shared `householdId`, assign it to the new owner, and backfill existing household members; added the `householdId(...)` helper.\n\n**Tests:** Full suite green \u2014 21 tests, 0 failures/errors (Cp10's `functionalityAllowsWithSharesHousehold` and Cp11's `coreJoinersShareHouseholdId` included).",
